WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.380
A methanogen and E. coli look almost the same under a microscope.

2
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:10.840
Are they close relatives?

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.200
Both are small cells with no nucleus.

4
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.140
A methanogen makes methane, and this one lives in a cow's rumen.

5
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.210
For a long time, biologists lumped all cells without a nucleus together as

6
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:29.070
prokaryotes. But that grouping rests on something missing.

7
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.270
Two cells can both lack a nucleus and still be distant relatives.

8
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:40.233
Carl Woese looked for a part that every cell carries, doing the same job in each.

9
00:00:41.473 --> 00:00:44.573
He chose the ribosome, the particle that makes proteins.

10
00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:48.993
Inside its small subunit lies a strand of RNA.

11
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:55.887
His laboratory pulled this small-subunit ribosomal RNA from many microbes.

12
00:00:57.187 --> 00:01:02.367
They cut each strand into pieces and compared the pieces, organism by organism.

13
00:01:03.827 --> 00:01:05.447
Each pair got a similarity score.

14
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:07.890
Here is the logic.

15
00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.810
Small changes pile up in the RNA as lineages separate.

16
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.030
So fewer differences usually mean a more recent common ancestor.

17
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:22.483
Now the scores fill a grid, one cell for every pair.

18
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:27.303
A stated grouping method can then propose a tree from the grid.

19
00:01:28.643 --> 00:01:33.023
Different methods make different assumptions, so the grid alone does not fix one

20
00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:34.023
tree.

21
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.427
Methanogens look like bacteria.

22
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:39.787
So where do you think they landed on the tree?

23
00:01:41.287 --> 00:01:44.507
Close to E. coli, or somewhere else?

24
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.490
Not among the bacteria.

25
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.590
Woese and George Fox reported that methanogens formed their own group.

26
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.590
It sat about as far from bacteria as from eukaryotes, the cells with a nucleus.

27
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:07.033
They first called the new group archaebacteria.

28
00:02:08.573 --> 00:02:13.593
Later, Woese and colleagues proposed three domains: Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya.

29
00:02:14.953 --> 00:02:16.993
Those names are still the standard labels.

30
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:20.527
What do the scores tell us, and what don't they?

31
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:22.787
They rank closeness.

32
00:02:23.987 --> 00:02:27.247
They do not date a split, and they do not reveal an ancestor.

33
00:02:28.517 --> 00:02:33.977
Across the deepest splits, repeated changes at one site can blur the signal in a

34
00:02:33.977 --> 00:02:39.697
single molecule. Many genes and better models of change sharpen the branching

35
00:02:39.697 --> 00:02:44.997
pattern. But which branch came off first also needs an outside reference.

36
00:02:46.117 --> 00:02:51.797
Most recent trees of this kind place eukaryotes inside the archaea, closest to a

37
00:02:51.797 --> 00:02:54.177
group called the Asgard archaea.

38
00:02:54.817 --> 00:02:56.337
Exactly where is still debated.

39
00:02:57.637 --> 00:03:01.937
On that view, life has two primary branches, Bacteria and Archaea.

40
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.690
So, back to the methanogen and E. coli.

41
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.870
Their looks hid how far apart they are.

42
00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.370
A molecule every cell shares revealed it.
